<spell-notebook "nag usage">
	<header level="1">
		Nag
		<header>A nag is a persistent message that stays until dismissed, and can remember the dismissal.</>
	</>
	<nag-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
			<item link="#using-it">Using it</>
			<item link="#remembering-the-dismissal">Remembering the dismissal</>
			<item link="#events">Events</>
			<item link="#behaviors">Behaviors</>
			<item link="#keyboard">Keyboard</>
			<item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
			<item link="#framework-usage">Framework usage</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  showing, remembering, events, script API, accessibility, frameworks. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			Nags present a one-time message that persists until the user acknowledges it: the site's use of cookies, an
			important change to the site. Write `<ui-nag>` with the text inside; there is no `.nag()` call to make.

			- It shows as soon as it is on the page, with a close icon (`closable`, on by default).
			- A `<span class="title">` inside is set off in the title colour.
			- Plain, it pushes the content down; `overlay` puts it over the top of its container, `fixed` pins it to the
			  top of the viewport, and `bottom` moves either to the bottom edge.
			- `display-time` hides it after that many milliseconds, without storing anything.
			- Start it `hidden` to show it later: `host.show()`, or a button with `commandfor` and `command="--show"`
			  (`--close` and `--toggle` work too).
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Remembering the dismissal">
		<markdown>
			Remembering is opt-in. With a `key`, closing the nag stores `value` (default `dismiss`) under that key in
			`storage`, so this browser doesn't get the nag again:

			- `storage`: `local` (`localStorage`), `session` (`sessionStorage`) or `cookie` (Fomantic's default, with
			  `path`, `domain`, `secure` and `samesite`)
			- `expires`: days the dismissal lasts (30 by default; `0` never expires)
			- a nag whose dismissal is stored is hidden before it paints, unless it's `persistent`
			- blocked storage (a private window, a strict browser) just doesn't remember: the nag still shows and closes
		</>

		<section label="Cookie nag" sticky collapsible dividing>
			<example "Cookie nag" description="Dismiss it, then reload:  it stays dismissed for this session (`storage=\"session\"`).">
				<nag key="site-cookie-nag" storage="session">
					<text class="title">We use cookies to ensure you get the best experience on our website</text>
				</nag>
			</example>
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			const nag = document.querySelector("ui-nag[key=site-cookie-nag]")
			nag.dismissed // true once dismissed, now or on an earlier visit
			nag.clear() // forget the dismissal ...
			nag.show() // ... and show it again
			```
		</>
		<message size="small" class="site-not-ported">Not ported: Fomantic's API-backed dismissal, and its expirationKey, detachable and context settings. The nag stays where it is written:  store the dismissal on a server from <text code>ui-closed</>.</>
	</>

	<section label="Events">
		<markdown>
			- `ui-opening` -- shown, its entry animation finished (Fomantic's `onVisible`); `detail` is `{}`.
			- `ui-closing` -- about to close, cancelable; `detail.reason` is `close` (the icon), `timeout` (`display-time`)
			  or `dismiss` (`host.close()`, an invoker command). `preventDefault()` keeps it and stores nothing
			  (Fomantic's `onHide` returning `false`).
			- `ui-closed` -- closed, its exit animation finished (Fomantic's `onHidden`); the host is `hidden` now.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			const nag = document.querySelector("ui-nag[key=cookies]")
			nag.addEventListener("ui-closing", (event) => {
			  if (!consentSaved) event.preventDefault() // keep it up, store nothing
			})
			nag.addEventListener("ui-closed", (event) => console.log(event.detail.reason))
			if (nag.dismissed) loadAnalytics()
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Behaviors">
		<markdown>
			Fomantic's behaviors are methods on the host:

			- `show` -- `nag.show()`: shows it again, unless a dismissal is stored (and it isn't `persistent`); true when it
			  shows
			- `hide` / `dismiss` -- `nag.close()`: closes it, storing the dismissal when it has a `key`
			- `clear` -- `nag.clear()`: forgets a stored dismissal
			- `nag.dismissed` -- true once dismissed, now or before
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>
			- **Tab** reaches the close icon, a real `<button>`.
			- **Enter** / **Space** on it closes the nag.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- A nag has no role of its own: it's content, read in page order. If it must be announced when it appears,
			  give it `role="status"` (or a `role="region"` with an `aria-label`, for a banner) yourself.
			- The close icon is a real button named "Close" (translated). With `closable="false"`, make sure something
			  else closes it.
			- A `fixed` nag covers the page's edge: leave room for it (padding on the body), so it never hides content.
			- If the element fails to render, it shows its text through a bare `<slot>`, with no close button:
			  hiding, `ui-closed`, remembering, `display-time` and the vetoes go.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Framework usage">
		<markdown>A nag manages itself: put it in the template with a `key`, and listen for `ui-closed` if you track it.</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-react">React 19</>
		<markdown>
			```jsx
			<ui-nag key="cookies" storage="local" onui-hide={() => setConsent(true)}>
			  We use cookies.
			</ui-nag>
			```
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-vue">Vue 3.5</>
		<markdown>
			```html
			<!-- compilerOptions.isCustomElement = (tag) => tag.startsWith("ui-") -->
			<ui-nag key="cookies" storage="local" @ui-closed="consent = true">We use cookies.</ui-nag>
			```
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-solid">Solid 2</>
		<markdown>
			```jsx
			// events and rich data are plain props:  `on-<event>={...}`, `options={...}`
			<ui-nag key="cookies" storage="local" on-ui-closed={() => setConsent(true)}>
			  We use cookies.
			</ui-nag>
			```
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
